### What does Java heap space mean?

The heap is where your objects live. When it is full and the garbage collector cannot free enough to satisfy an allocation, you get this error. The cause is usually a genuine leak (holding references you no longer need) or an undersized heap.

```java
package com.bank.diagnostic;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class HeapSpaceDemo {
    // WRONG: static list grows without bound
    private static final List<BigDecimal> statementCache = new ArrayList<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Simulate leaking statement data
        for (int i = 0; i < Integer.MAX_VALUE; i++) {
            statementCache.add(new BigDecimal("1000.00"));
        }
    }
}
```

```bash
java -Xmx64m com.bank.diagnostic.HeapSpaceDemo
```

```text
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space
    at java.util.Arrays.copyOf(Arrays.java:3210)
    at java.util.ArrayList.grow(ArrayList.java:265)
    at com.bank.diagnostic.HeapSpaceDemo.main(HeapSpaceDemo.java:12)
```

```bash
# Diagnose with a heap dump
jmap -dump:live,format=b,file=heap.hprof <pid>
# Or automatically:
java -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError -XX:HeapDumpPath=/tmp/heap.hprof ...
```

### What does GC overhead limit exceeded mean?

The throughput collector throws this when it spends over 98% of time in GC while recovering less than 2% of the heap. It is effectively a thrashing heap.

```java
package com.bank.diagnostic;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class GcOverheadDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<Integer, byte[]> map = new HashMap<>();
        // Fill the heap so GC thrashes
        for (int i = 0; i < Integer.MAX_VALUE; i++) {
            map.put(i, new byte[1024]); // 1 KB per entry
        }
    }
}
```

```bash
java -Xmx32m com.bank.diagnostic.GcOverheadDemo
```

```text
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: GC overhead limit exceeded
```

The root cause and fix are the same as Java heap space: find the retained references with a heap dump.

### What does Metaspace mean?

Metaspace stores class metadata in native memory (since Java 8, replacing PermGen). The usual cause is a classloader leak from repeated redeploys or heavy dynamic class generation.

```java
package com.bank.diagnostic;

import java.net.URL;
import java.net.URLClassLoader;

public class MetaspaceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Simulate classloader leak: load classes without releasing classloaders
        while (true) {
            URLClassLoader loader = new URLClassLoader(
                    new URL[]{new URL("file:///tmp/classes/")});
            loader.loadClass("com.bank.DynamicClass");
            // Leak: never close the loader, classes stay in Metaspace
        }
    }
}
```

```bash
java -XX:MaxMetaspaceSize=64m com.bank.diagnostic.MetaspaceDemo
```

```text
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace
```

```bash
# Monitor loaded class count
jcmd <pid> VM.classloader_stats
# A steadily rising class count across redeploys = classloader leak
```

### What does Direct buffer memory mean?

Off-heap direct `ByteBuffer`s used by NIO and Netty exceeded the limit. These buffers are freed only when their Java wrapper is garbage-collected and the cleaner runs.

```java
package com.bank.diagnostic;

import java.nio.ByteBuffer;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class DirectBufferDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<ByteBuffer> buffers = new ArrayList<>();
        // Allocate direct buffers without releasing
        while (true) {
            buffers.add(ByteBuffer.allocateDirect(1024 * 1024)); // 1 MB
        }
    }
}
```

```bash
java -XX:MaxDirectMemorySize=64m com.bank.diagnostic.DirectBufferDemo
```

```text
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Direct buffer memory
```

A heap dump shows nothing for this error because the memory is off-heap. Use Native Memory Tracking instead.

### What does unable to create native thread mean?

This is not about heap at all. The OS refused a new thread, usually because the process hit a thread or memory limit.

```java
package com.bank.diagnostic;

public class ThreadLeakDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create threads without limit
        while (true) {
            new Thread(() -> {
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(Long.MAX_VALUE);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                }
            }).start();
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.OutOfMemoryError:
    unable to create native thread: possibly out of memory or
    process/resource limits reached
```

```bash
# Fix: reduce threads, pool them, lower stack size, raise OS limits
java -Xss256k ...              # smaller stack per thread
ulimit -u 65535                # raise process limit
```

### What does Compressed class space mean?

On 64-bit JVMs, compressed class pointers use a capped region. When it fills, raise `CompressedClassSpaceSize` or reduce loaded classes.

```bash
java -XX:CompressedClassSpaceSize=32m ...
# Error:
# java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Compressed class space

# Fix:
java -XX:CompressedClassSpaceSize=256m ...
```

### Which tool do you use for each error?

| Error | Region | Tool |
|---|---|---|
| Java heap space | Heap | Heap dump + Eclipse MAT |
| GC overhead limit | Heap | Heap dump + MAT |
| Metaspace | Native (class metadata) | jcmd classloader stats, class count |
| Direct buffer memory | Native (off-heap) | NMT, BufferPool MBean |
| unable to create native thread | OS | Thread dump, ulimit, pmap |
| Compressed class space | Native (class pointers) | jcmd, class count |

```bash
# Enable Native Memory Tracking
java -XX:NativeMemoryTracking=summary ...

# Query it
jcmd <pid> VM.native_memory summary
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The message is a map, not a diagnosis. Heap errors point to the Java heap and need a heap dump. Metaspace and compressed-class errors point to classloading and need class-count tracking. Direct memory and native-thread errors point to native memory and the OS, where a heap dump shows nothing.

```java
package com.bank.diagnostic;

import java.lang.management.BufferPoolMXBean;
import java.lang.management.ManagementFactory;

public class DirectBufferMonitor {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Monitor direct buffer usage
        for (BufferPoolMXBean pool :
                ManagementFactory.getPlatformMXBeans(BufferPoolMXBean.class)) {
            if ("direct".equals(pool.getName())) {
                System.out.println("Direct buffers: " + pool.getCount());
                System.out.println("Memory used: " + pool.getMemoryUsed());
                System.out.println("Total capacity: " + pool.getTotalCapacity());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Direct buffers: 12
Memory used: 12582912
Total capacity: 12582912
```
